<mj-section background-color="#eff1f4" padding="1px 1px 1px 1px" border-radius="10px">
  <mj-column background-color="#fff" border-radius="10px" padding="0px">
    <mj-image
      border-radius="10px 10px 0 0"
      padding="0"
      width="600px"
      src="https://www.londonsummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
      alt="Shakespeare’s site fixed"
      href="https://www.londonsummary.com/"
    />
    <mj-text
      padding="20px 20px 0px 20px"
      font-family="Austin News Text Web, TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif"
      color="#000000"
    >
      <h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 400; margin: 0;">
        Shakespeare’s site fixed
      </h2>
    </mj-text>
    <mj-text padding="0px 20px 0px 20px" font-family="Arial" color="#000000">
      <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; padding-top: 15px; margin: 0;">
  <strong>What’s happening? </strong>Researchers have pinpointed the exact location of <a href="https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cedeenvdwgxo" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #80011F; color: black;">William Shakespeare’s known London property in Blackfriars</a>, using a newly identified floor plan and supporting archival documents. The discovery also reveals the property was larger and more substantial than previously understood. Scholars say this could reshape assumptions about Shakespeare’s later years, suggesting he may have spent more time in London and possibly even worked from the house after buying it in 1613.
</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; padding-top: 15px; margin: 0;">
  <strong>Details:</strong> The breakthrough came after Professor Lucy Munro identified three overlooked documents, including a 1668 post-Great Fire plan that mapped the size and position of Shakespeare’s Blackfriars property. The house was part of the Great Gate at the entrance to the former Dominican friary and stretched across what are currently Ireland Yard, Burgon Street, and parts of nearby later buildings. Two additional records traced the property’s sale by Shakespeare’s granddaughter in 1665.
</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 24px; padding-top: 15px; padding-bottom: 20px; margin: 0;">
  <strong>Big picture:</strong> Historians had often treated the Blackfriars purchase as a late investment made before he withdrew to Stratford. But the newly identified floor plan suggests the property was larger, more valuable, and more closely tied to his theatrical world than once thought. That makes it easier to imagine Shakespeare still actively living, writing, or managing business in London after 1613.
</p>
    </mj-text>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>
<mj-spacer height="20px" />
    
    <mj-section background-color="#eff1f4" padding="1px 1px 1px 1px" border-radius="10px">
  <mj-column background-color="#fff" border-radius="10px" padding="0px">
    <mj-text padding="20px 20px 0px 20px"
      font-family="Austin News Text Web, TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif"
      color="#000000">
      <h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 400; margin: 0;">Long story short</h2>
    </mj-text>
    <mj-divider border-width="4.8px" border-color="#80011F" width="35px" align="left" padding="0 20px 0px 20px" />
    <mj-spacer height="30px" />

    <mj-text padding="0px 20px 10px 20px" font-family="Arial" color="#000000">
  <h3 style="font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 700; margin: 0;">Business</h3>
</mj-text>
<mj-table
  font-family="Arial"
  cellpadding="0"
  cellspacing="0"
  padding="0px 32px 5px 32px"
  style="width: 100%"
>
  <tr>
  <td style="
    font-size: 18px;
    vertical-align: top;
    line-height: 24px;
    padding-bottom: 15px;
    padding-right: 8px;
  "> &#8226; </td>
  <td style="
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 24px;
    padding-bottom: 15px;
    padding-left: 0px;
  ">
    <a href="https://www.thenews.com.pk/latest/1399190-uk-faces-potential-food-shortages-as-iran-conflict-drags-on" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #80011F; color: black;">The UK government is preparing contingency plans for a worst-case food supply shock if the Iran conflict leads to a prolonged closure of the Strait of Hormuz</a>. Officials are particularly concerned about disruptions to carbon dioxide supplies, which are essential for food preservation and animal slaughter. Industry leaders are split on the likely impact: some warn more about inflation than outright shortages, while others want full contingency planning.
  </td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td style="
    font-size: 18px;
    vertical-align: top;
    line-height: 24px;
    padding-bottom: 15px;
    padding-right: 8px;
  "> &#8226; </td>
  <td style="
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 24px;
    padding-bottom: 15px;
    padding-left: 0px;
  ">
    <a href="https://www.cnbc.com/2026/04/16/fca-uk-hedge-funds-investors-short-sellers-red-tape-rules-regulation-financial-conduct-authority.html" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #80011F; color: black;">The UK’s Financial Conduct Authority has overhauled its short-selling rules</a>. From July 13, the FCA will stop publicly identifying individual investors with net short positions and instead publish only aggregated company-level data. The regulator says the new framework is simpler and more workable while still preserving enough oversight to keep markets fair.
  </td>
</tr>
</mj-table>

  </mj-column>
</mj-section>
<mj-spacer height="20px" />
